<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Unity custom main manifest.
    Requires Player Settings > Publishing Settings > Custom Main Manifest = ON.

    This file REPLACES Unity's UnityManifest.xml template wholesale and becomes
    unityLibrary/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml.

    ===========================================================================
    DECLARE EXACTLY ONE <activity>, AND IT MUST MATCH
    Player Settings > Other Settings > Application Entry Point.
    ===========================================================================

    Two separate failures hang off this, one from each direction:

    1. Declare the WRONG one. Unity post-processes this file and stamps
       android:enabled="false" on the activity that does not match the entry
       point setting, leaving nothing launchable:

           DeploymentOperationFailedException: No activity in the manifest with
           action MAIN and category LAUNCHER

    2. Declare BOTH (the obvious "make it work either way" fix). The build then
       succeeds and installs, but the app opens as a flat 2D panel in Horizon
       Home instead of launching immersively. OpenXR's Meta Quest Support
       feature injects the VR markers via
       Editor/MetaQuest/ModifyAndroidManifestMeta.cs:

           ActivityIntentFilterElement =
               SelectSingleNode("/manifest/application/activity/intent-filter")
           ActivityElement =
               SelectSingleNode("manifest/application/activity")

       SelectSingleNode returns the FIRST match, so
       com.oculus.intent.category.VR and com.oculus.vr.focusaware land on
       whichever activity you listed first. If that is the disabled one, the
       live activity never becomes a VR entry point and Horizon OS treats the
       app as flat.

    CURRENTLY SET TO: GameActivity (androidApplicationEntry: 2).
    UnityPlayerGameActivity is declared below; UnityPlayerActivity is not.

    Player Settings > Other Settings > Application Entry Point MUST be set to
    "GameActivity" to match. BEFORE changing this file to match a different
    Player Setting, check ProjectSettings/ProjectSettings.asset for
    androidApplicationEntry (1 = Activity, 2 = GameActivity, 3 = both - never
    use 3, see failure mode #2 above) - this exact mismatch is what caused a
    "No activity with action MAIN and category LAUNCHER" failure once already.

    If you switch the entry point to Activity, swap the block below for
    com.unity3d.player.UnityPlayerActivity with theme @style/UnityThemeSelector
    and drop the android.app.lib_name meta-data.

    ===========================================================================
    Everything VR-specific is deliberately left to the XR package. Do NOT add
    com.oculus.supportedDevices or <uses-feature android.hardware.vr.headtracking>
    here - ModifyAndroidManifestMeta.cs writes both, and declaring them yourself
    collides with :unityLibrary:xrmanifest.androidlib:

        Manifest merger failed : Attribute meta-data#com.oculus.supportedDevices
        @value value=(quest3|quest3s) from [:unityLibrary] is also present at
        [:unityLibrary:xrmanifest.androidlib] value=(quest|quest2|cambria|eureka|quest3s)

    Also omitted on purpose: android:label / android:icon (Unity's
    LauncherManifest.xml sets them) and unity.splash-* (injected by the build).
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
          xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />
     <uses-permission android:name="horizonos.permission.READ_SETTINGS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="horizonos.permission.WRITE_SETTINGS" />
    <!--
        THE ONE THING THIS FILE ADDS BEYOND UNITY'S DEFAULT.

        GeckoBootstrapApplication's static initialiser loads
        libgeckovulkanbridge.so at process startup - before UnityPlayer builds
        the VkDevice, which is the only window in which vkCreateDevice can be
        intercepted to add the AHardwareBuffer extensions. Miss it and
        GetVulkanImagePointer returns null.

        Safe to declare here: Unity's LauncherManifest.xml sets only
        android:label and android:icon on <application>, no android:name, so
        there is nothing higher-priority to lose to.
    -->
    <application android:name="com.example.geckovulkan.GeckoBootstrapApplication"
                 android:extractNativeLibs="true"
                  android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
                  android:largeHeap="true">

        <!-- Matches androidApplicationEntry: 2 (GameActivity). See header. -->

        <!-- largeHeap: the cinema system now brings a theater's WebView +
             AHardwareBuffer + Vulkan-imported texture up and back down again
             every time the player changes theaters (GeckoVulkanRenderer's
             BeginInitialise/ShutdownBridge), instead of the single
             initialize-once-for-the-app-lifetime pattern this bridge shipped
             with. Each cycle allocates and frees a same-sized native buffer
             (e.g. 1920x1080 RGBA8 is ~8MB, before whatever GeckoView's own
             process needs), and Android's default per-process heap cap makes
             repeated churn like that a real OOM risk over a long session with
             several theater switches. This does NOT touch the AAR - it's a
             manifest flag entirely on the app-shell side. -->

        <!-- hardwareAccelerated MUST stay true while the browser is an
             android.webkit.WebView. With it false the whole View hierarchy is
             software-rendered, which (a) makes Widevine/EME refuse to initialise -
             the CDM needs a GPU-composited path, and surfaces it to JS as
             "OperationError: Non-recoverable error. Do not retry." - and
             (b) starves Chromium's tile manager, hence "tile memory limits
             exceeded, some content may not draw". Unity's own Vulkan output does
             not go through the Android Canvas, so this does not affect the bridge. -->
        <activity android:name="com.unity3d.player.UnityPlayerGameActivity"
                  android:theme="@style/BaseUnityGameActivityTheme"
                  android:launchMode="singleTask"
                  android:configChanges="mcc|mnc|locale|touchscreen|keyboard|keyboardHidden|navigation|orientation|screenLayout|uiMode|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|fontScale|layoutDirection|density"
                  android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
                  android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data android:name="unityplayer.UnityActivity" android:value="true" />
            <meta-data android:name="android.app.lib_name" android:value="game" />
        </activity>
    </application>
</manifest>
